{"results":[{"url":"https://schengenvisainfo.com/news/fact-check-did-france-cancel-visas-of-183-pakistanis-due-to-the-french-pakistan-row/","final_url":"https://schengenvisainfo.com/news/fact-check-did-france-cancel-visas-of-183-pakistanis-due-to-the-french-pakistan-row/","title":"Fact Check: Did France Cancel Visas of 183 Pakistanis Due to the French-Pakistan Row","description":"A report published on Sunday claiming that France has cancelled visitor visas of 183 citizens of Pakistan has been on the spotlight of international media these days. Such a decision was reported that allegedly happened soon after Pakistan’s Prime Minister Imran Khan criticized French President Emmanuel Macron over his comments on Islamophobia, . The information […]","language":"en","text":"Statistics\n\n# Fact Check: Did France Cancel Visas of 183 Pakistanis Due to the French-Pakistan Row\n\nNovember 2, 2020\n\nA report published on Sunday claiming that France has cancelled visitor visas of 183 citizens of Pakistan has been on the spotlight of international media these days.\n\nSuch a decision was reported that allegedly happened soon after Pakistan’s Prime Minister Imran Khan criticized French President Emmanuel Macron over his comments on Islamophobia, .\n\nThe information was spread by a Twitter account named “the Pakistan Consulate in Paris” under the handler @PakConsulateFr, which also claimed that among the persons whose visa France cancelled was also the sister of former chief of Pakistan’s intelligence agency ISI, Lt Gen Ahmed Shuja Pasha.\n\nAccording to the same, the Pakistan consulate in Paris requested France to permit the ISI Chief’s sister to temporarily remain in the country as she came to France to visit her sick mother-in-law.\n\nIn addition, the consulate also announced that the 118 nationals were deported by force, despite the fact that they owned valid documents.\n\nPakistan’s Embassy in France has immediately reacted to this information, calling it fake, and clarifying that no such thing has happened.\n\n## So, Did France Cancel the Cisas of 183 Pakistanis?\n\nNo, the Embassy of Pakistan in Paris did not cancel visas for 183 citizens of Pakistan, as it was reported by a large number of media worldwide.\n\nThe Embassy of Pakistan in Paris stressed in a notice published to clarify the issue that France has only one official account on Twitter, calling the other account fake and misleading.\n\nAt the same time, the official Twitter account of Pakistan’s Embassy in Paris urged all persons to report the fake account in order to avoid the spreading of fake news and false information.\n\n*“The Embassy of Pakistan in Paris, France has only one Twitter account @PakInFrance. Any other account claiming to represent the Embassy anywhere in France is fake & misleading & may be reported to @Twitter,”* the Embassy of Pakistan stated on its Twitter account.\n\nA day before, the  Twitter account  @PakConsulateFr, which pretended to be the account of Consulate General Of Pakistan France, stressed that on Saturday that visitor visas of 118 Pakistanis were cancelled by the French authorities after Prime Minister Imran Khan’s comments.\n\n*“183 visitor visas provided to our citizens have been found to be rejected by French authorities following the criticism by PM Imran khan. 118 citizens with proper documents were deported forcefully. We’re currently in touch with french authority to give our citizens a temporary stay,”* the fake tweets read.\n\nA fake Twitter account claiming to be the Consulate General of Pakistan in France has spread fake news over the recent days\n\n### Macron’s Comments Which Led to Anti-France Protests in Pakistan\n\nA large number of Pakistani citizens have marched on Pakistan’s streets, to demonstrate against the republishing of caricatures depicting the Prophet Muhammad.\n\nThe tensions have increased after President Emmanuel Macron’s comments, adding France would not “*renounce the caricatures*”. He also defended freedoms of expression in France.\n\nIn an interview made on Sunday, explained that some incorrect translations of his declarations in the media made him look like he supported the cartoons mocking Prophet Muhammad.\n\n*“I have never said that. Those are lies,”* Macron said during the interview for Al-Jazeera.\n\nSmaller protests have followed Pakistan’s protests in other countries, such as Turkey, Lebanon and India.\n\n#### Top Stories\n\nThe European Union's single online platform for Schengen visa applications is expected to start operating around 2030, according to...","author":"Shkurta Januzi","published_date":"2020-11-02T16:22:37+00:00","latency_ms":3086.718710983405,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}